# A green run

Cheapest first: every next check is only worth running once the one before it passes.

```bash
uv run ruff check . --fix             # --fix first: half the failures fix themselves
uv run ruff format .                  # the gate is `ruff format --check .`; this command satisfies it
uv run mypy .
uv run lint-imports
uv run pytest -m "not integration"
{% if include_codegen %}uv run python -m tools.codegen --check
{% endif %}```

"Done" is said after all of them pass, not after the last edit.

## What a failure means

**`I001` in a file that imports `composition`** — `composition` is its own isort section, and
sections are separated by a blank line. `ruff check --fix` fixes it.

**`S101` outside `tests/`** — `assert` is allowed only in tests. In code it is a check and an
exception.

**`T201`** — `print` is allowed only in an entrypoint under `composition/`. No layer below it
prints.

**mypy complains about a generated file** (the "Generated by" header) — change the specification
and regenerate; that file is never edited by hand, the next run overwrites it.

**`lint-imports`** — the code sits in the wrong layer. Move the code; leave the contract in
`pyproject.toml` alone.

**A test from `tests/architecture` fails** — fix the code. Only a human writes `arc: ignore`:
if it cannot be fixed, stop and describe the violation and the rule to the user.

**Any other test fails** — first decide which is wrong: the test or the code. Weakening,
skipping or deleting a test to make the suite pass is forbidden.

**A test needs live services** — everything in `tests/infrastructure` is marked `integration`
automatically and stays out of `-m "not integration"`. Run them by hand:
`uv run pytest -m integration`.

**`ModuleNotFoundError` on your own dependency** — `pyproject.toml` changed, so `uv sync` is due.
{% if include_codegen %}
**`generated code is out of date`** — run `uv run python -m tools.codegen` and commit the result.

**The generator exits with code 2** — the specification is wrong; the message names the file and
the key. `spec.schema.json` next to it checks the structure, and the README in the same folder
describes the language.
{% endif %}
